	<article class="subpost" aria-labelledby="scoutingplantitforward-label" id="scoutingplantitforward">
		{{ websiteCarousel([
			{ url: "https://web.archive.org/web/20260818005234if_/https://scoutingplantitforward.org/", alt: "The Scouting Plant It Forward homepage, with a forest background reading \"Plant It Forward. Support a Scout. Create a Legacy.\" next to a stat reading \"Because of you, 787 Trees Planted,\"" }
		]) }}
		<h3 id="scoutingplantitforward-label">Scouting Plant It Forward</h3>
		<h4><a href="https://scoutingplantitforward.org/" rel="external" target="_blank">Website</a></h4>
		<p>A fundraising website for Northern Star Scouting, offering an alternative to the traditional popcorn sale: instead of a product, donors fund a native tree planted in the Superior National Forest through the Arbor Day Foundation, keeping the benefit local to the community that donates. Proceeds are split evenly between the participating Scout and the council, which covers card-processing fees and tree-planting logistics.</p>
		<h4>Technologies</h4>
		<p>ScoutingPlantItForward.org starts from the same Eleventy (11ty) static build as this site, but layers a Cloudflare Workers backend on top to handle logins, payments, and reporting:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Payments run through Clover's iframe integration, so card details are entered directly into a Clover-hosted frame by the user and never touch the site's servers. As it only ever receives a payment token back, the PCI DSS burden is much lower than actually natively processing payments.</li>
			<li>Cloudflare Turnstile guards the donate and checkout forms after Clover's own bot protection didn't actually work - it just put up a recaptcha logo but never ran any scripts or checks.</li>
			<li>A full account system backs Scout, leader, and admin logins. Cloudflare D1 stores users, units (packs/troops), and transactions, while Cloudflare KV stores sessions, checked on every (secured) page load (i.e. dashboards) so a stolen or edited cookie alone can't forge a session. Passwords are salted and hashed with PBKDF2-SHA256, and forgotten-password requests first store a secret in D1, then hand off emailing to an n8n webhook to email a magic reset link that verifies against the secret in D1 server-side.</li>
			<li>Every page except the home page is deliberately named <code>template.html</code> instead of <code>index.html</code>. Cloudflare Workers with static assets skips running the Worker entirely whenever a request matches an existing static file (for speed and to save on Worker invocations), so forcing a 404 on the "pretty" URL is what lets the Worker step in to inject login state or dashboard data, while every other request still gets served straight from the fast static cache.</li>
			<li>An n8n workflow triggers a Cloudflare deploy hook every night, rebuilding the site so data like the homepage's "trees planted" counter stays current even on days with no code changes.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>It has a private GitHub repository.</p>
